WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.840
- Hi, everybody.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.040
I'm Maurice Samuels, the Director

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.330
of the Yale Program for
the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.120
and it's my great pleasure
to welcome you here today

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.300
to our Benjamin and Barbara
Zucker Lecture Series,

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.140
the first one of 2022.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.400
Please make sure to sign up

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.390
for the YPSA mailing
list on the YPSA website.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.680
We have a lot of great talks
planned for this semester.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.640
So, today's speaker, Gregory Jones-Katz

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.010
is an American historian based

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.780
at the Chinese University
of Hong Kong, Shenzhen.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.550
He earned his PhD from the
University of Wisconsin-Madison.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.300
Greg works in the fields
of American intellectual

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.710
and cultural history, the
history of higher education,

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.390
and the global history of the humanities.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.620
His first book "Deconstruction:
An American Institution"

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.287
was published in 2021, so just recently,

00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:05.300
with the University of Chicago Press.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.273
Greg's scholarly work has also appeared

00:01:07.273 --> 00:01:10.857
in "History and Theory,"
"Analyse &amp; Kritik,"

00:01:10.857 --> 00:01:14.280
"Rethinking History" and
"Modern Intellectual History."

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.920
He's committed to public
facing scholarship

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.910
and has written for the Los
Angeles Review of Books,

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.910
the Chronicle of Higher Education

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.700
and other mainstream outlets.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.510
So just a word about how
things are gonna work today,

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.120
Greg will speak for about 35 minutes.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.540
Then we'll open it up for
questions from the audience.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.685
Please submit your questions
using the Q&amp;A feature

00:01:36.685 --> 00:01:39.760
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.480
The chat function is
disabled and I can't see you.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.120
So I won't know if you raise your hand,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.641
but I will ask questions that you submit

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.530
using the Q &amp; A function.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.380
We'll end promptly at 6:00 PM.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.977
Greg's title today is
"Challenging Humanism:

00:01:57.977 --> 00:02:01.574
"Jews, Theory and Yale
During the Closing Decades

00:02:01.574 --> 00:02:03.760
of the 20th Century."

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.543
So please join me in welcoming Greg.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:10.493
Come on.

00:02:10.493 --> 00:02:11.326
Terrific, hi.

00:02:11.326 --> 00:02:13.690
Okay, I'm gonna mute myself
now and you can take it away.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.800
- Thank you.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.911
Thank you, Maurice, for
inviting me tonight for my talk,

00:02:19.911 --> 00:02:23.160
thank you to the Yale Program
for the Study of Antisemitism"

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.803
and thank you to everybody in attendance.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.550
I'm going to be speaking tonight

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.280
about how Jews deployed theory at Yale.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.990
This paper is derived
from a forthcoming article

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.860
in the journals "Jewish Social Studies,"

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.320
and it's part of an evolving

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.210
and larger study on the age of theory,

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.623
its production, consumption
and dissemination.

00:02:44.915 --> 00:02:47.730
Informed of changes at Yale University,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.970
and across American higher education,

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.860
then decentering the white male

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.870
and Christian European perspective,

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.970
pioneering Jews in the
late 1960s and 1970s

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.550
first reworked philosophical principles

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.570
of literary scholarship, and
then helped inaugurate an array

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.630
of curricular and intellectual changes.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.980
With their anti-humanist,
often de-Christianizing work,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.180
Jews at Yale theorized and while doing so,

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.330
also enlarged the purview
of humanities programs

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.820
and aided the establishment
of various departments

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.823
and fields of study.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.160
This story is important

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.980
for several historiographical reasons.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.916
Dan Oren, in 1985, provided a rich history

00:03:25.916 --> 00:03:29.200
of how Jews strove to,
quote, "Join the club."

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.750
Building on Oren's
study, this talk explores

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.290
the intellectual consequences
of part of this movement.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.201
This talk also explores how
Jews' curricular expansions

00:03:38.201 --> 00:03:41.470
in the 1970s contributed
to what David Hollinger

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.260
has described as the
desacralization of academic culture.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.610
Finally, I aim to illuminate
how Jews' deployments of theory

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.770
shaped humanistic tenants then informed

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.230
the Americanization of the Holocaust,

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.520
a process through which the
Holocaust was established

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.300
as a key reference point in
American culture and became

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.603
a central event of modern
American Jewish history.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.770
By the mid 20th century, a
Christian Protestant culture

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.880
and norms was entrenched
in American universities.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.180
From the 1970s and the 1980s,
Jews at Yale developed theory

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.030
from this social standpoint of exclusion

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.177
as a tool to challenge the existing order.

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:22.427
"Theory," Gerald Graff has observed,

00:04:22.427 --> 00:04:26.197
"is what erupts when what
was once silently agreed to

00:04:26.197 --> 00:04:28.267
"in a community becomes disputed,

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:31.540
"forcing its members to formulate
and defend assumptions."

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.587
Theory here, in this talk,
denotes that Jews questioned

00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:37.859
and then reformulated about
humanism, but not just making

00:04:37.859 --> 00:04:40.600
the explicit of
assumptions about humanism.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.890
Theory is also a way
of interacting with it,

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:45.330
which produces new knowledge

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.210
and does not conserve traditions.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.020
As such, Jews' challenges
of humanism at Yale

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.400
by using theory were anti-humanist,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.080
though these challenges
generated knowledge

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.450
that reformed established
anti-humanist traditions.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.530
This project was sometimes
thus an interpretive mode.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:04.330
And sometimes, a mood
emanating from exclusion

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.203
and the marginalization as Jews.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.560
By the mid 1960s, Western
intellectuals were exposing

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:12.645
the idea of universal man to be part

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.370
of Christian European ideology.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.590
While authors pondered this crisis of man,

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.350
Geoffrey Hartman, who many
here have, perhaps, heard of,

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.560
and some here worked with,
I had the honor of spending

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.880
several afternoons talking to
him about his life and work,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.310
while pondering this crisis of man,

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:31.960
Geoffrey Hartman explored the humanism

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.620
espoused in poetry and prose.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:36.660
A half of century later,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.100
his death in 1916 was widely observed.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.820
It was noted Hartman's
birth in Frankfurt, Germany,

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.610
on August 11th, 1929, how he was sent

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.010
on Kindertransport by his
family to England in 1939,

00:05:50.152 --> 00:05:52.620
how he was reunited with his mother

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.955
in New York City in 1946,
eventually attending Yale

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:59.190
where he was a faculty
member for four decades.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.807
Hartman, quote, "Took
in the romantic poets,

00:06:01.807 --> 00:06:04.167
"Judaic sacred texts, Holocaust studies,

00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:06.397
"deconstruction, the workings of memory,

00:06:06.397 --> 00:06:09.280
"even the very function
of criticism itself."

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.700
But looking back on his
career in the early 2000s,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.007
Hartman observed, quote,

00:06:13.007 --> 00:06:15.237
"I suspect I am, at heart, an essayist,

00:06:15.237 --> 00:06:16.287
"always finding something

00:06:16.287 --> 00:06:18.527
"of basic human and social interest."

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.470
The circumstances for this kind of work

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.180
were far from propitious.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.840
At the end of the 19th
century, Jews were excluded

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.390
from English departments,

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.990
whose members often thought
themselves as defenders of,

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.800
quote-unquote, "civilization."

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.210
With the study of English literature,

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.610
as a way to inculcate culture, that is,

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.760
Anglo-Saxon Christianity.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.780
Antisemitism remained rampant
during the interwar years,

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.652
but its decline in
explicitness after World War II

00:06:43.652 --> 00:06:47.430
helped open opportunities for Jewish men.

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:50.511
Jews at Yale did not fare well.

00:06:50.511 --> 00:06:54.140
True, since the university's
establishment in 1701,

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.430
Hebrew was required and from the 1800s,

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.020
Jews attended and worked there.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.970
Hartman was not the first tenured Jew

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.520
in Yale's English department,
though the university

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.670
did maintain a quote-unquote
"genteel resistance to Jews"

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.130
after the end of the second World War.

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:11.140
Three academic generations
would combat the exclusion

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:14.520
of quote-unquote "the
Jew" from humanist ideals.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.220
This project's prospects
were initially unclear

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:21.220
when members of Yale English
aligned literary education

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.640
with the inculcation of Christian beliefs.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.180
Before World War II,
Professor Chauncey Tinker,

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.620
quote, "summarily vetoed consideration"

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.970
of Jewish-American Lionel
Trilling for professorship.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.697
Jews, Tinker decided, quote,

00:07:33.697 --> 00:07:35.637
"lacked the cultural
and religious background

00:07:35.637 --> 00:07:38.187
"necessary for the teaching
of English literature."

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:43.610
The inclusion of Jews in
Yale English was slow.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.950
Impetus came from legislation such

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.270
as Connecticut anti-discrimination
laws in the 1960s

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.090
and the 1964 Civil Rights Act.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.870
A more approximate cause for changes

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.590
came from Kingman Brewster who,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:01.810
appointed president of
Yale of University in '63,

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.480
asked admission staff to remove

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.690
the last relics of
discrimination against Jews

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:07.230
and other minorities.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.320
The transformation of
the student body started

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.100
with the university's
recruitment from public schools.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.067
By the late 1960s, the
homogenous quote-unquote,

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:17.740
"Yale undergraduate experience" was over,

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:21.300
but that was all long way off
when Charles Feidelson, Jr.,

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.050
the first tenured Jewish
scholar in Yale English,

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:26.197
challenged in a '48 book,

00:08:26.197 --> 00:08:27.760
"Narratives of American Literature"

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.930
that justified the self
in Puritan identities.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:33.420
But it was Harold Bloom,
close friends with Hartman

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:34.940
and who become one of the most famous

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:37.480
American literary critics
who first directly,

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:40.050
while Yale English's second
tenured Jewish critic,

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:42.430
challenged the Christian man constructed

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:45.230
out of Yale literary formalism.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.200
Born in 1930, Bloom was raised

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.750
in a Yiddish speaking
household in the Bronx.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:51.450
After receiving his undergraduate degree

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.930
from Cornell in '51, the
self-proclaimed street fighter

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.550
earned his PhD in Yale English in 1955.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.057
Bloom called the department quote,

00:09:00.057 --> 00:09:02.027
"an Anglo-Catholic nightmare.

00:09:02.027 --> 00:09:04.657
"I walked out of a
lecture," he once wrote,

00:09:04.657 --> 00:09:06.797
"by Tinker soon after it began,

00:09:06.797 --> 00:09:08.647
"because it devoted
itself to the assertion

00:09:08.647 --> 00:09:10.837
"that John Milton's poem, in every way,

00:09:10.837 --> 00:09:13.970
"transcended the barbaric Hebrew text."

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.020
Bloom responded and his subversions

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.120
of Yale New Critics' work
were part of a movement

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.420
by Jewish scholars who seemed
to read their own situation

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:23.730
into the romantic
sympathies with outcasts,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.920
exiles, and outsiders.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:28.410
In Shelley's "Myth
Making," published in '59,

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.870
Bloom embraced Jewish
philosopher Martin Buber's

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.212
I-Thou concept of relationships to trace

00:09:33.212 --> 00:09:38.010
the mythopoetic power of
Percy Shelley's poems.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.680
Buber provided a foundation
for Jewish humanism

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.670
that countered the prevailing
Anglo-Christian humanism.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:46.027
This alternative stressed how all, quote,

00:09:46.027 --> 00:09:48.797
"philosophical, naturalistic,
humanistic ethics

00:09:48.797 --> 00:09:51.557
"are inadequate, that
Jewish religious texts offer

00:09:51.557 --> 00:09:53.917
"a more compelling basis
for ethical conduct."

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.700
Entering Yale's Comparative
Literature doctoral program

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:02.100
in the early 1950s, Geoffrey
Hartman similarly aimed

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.380
to antithetically navigate Yale formalism

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:05.980
and its Christian culture.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.540
The measure Hartman judged
Yale English as possessing

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.890
a quote, "severe evangelical atmosphere."

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.440
Coming up through comp lit,
comparative literature,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.450
and interested in European poetics,

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:20.447
Hartman appreciated Yale
New Criticism as quote,

00:10:20.447 --> 00:10:22.937
"something small within
a much larger pedagogical

00:10:22.937 --> 00:10:24.690
"and cultural enterprise."

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:27.420
His graduate education instilled
students with the belief

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.900
in the harmony of
European humanist culture

00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:33.870
and the need to quote, "affirm
its literary inheritance."

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.700
In "The Unmediated
Vision," published in 1954,

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.838
Hartman read passages from
European poets to show that

00:10:39.838 --> 00:10:43.327
a, quote, "common striving
for a poetry which mediated

00:10:43.327 --> 00:10:45.740
"between its readers
and direct experience"

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.010
was a modern development
in literary history,

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.323
one that Yale's New Critics ignored.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.420
But the Christian inflected
song remained the same

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:56.730
when he started teaching at Yale in 1955,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.220
with restrictions on the number
of Jewish students enforced

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.590
under an admissions quota and excluding

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.510
one secular philosopher,
no tenured Jewish teachers

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:06.590
in the school's humanities departments.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.920
Hartman's next book rethought
parameters of humanism.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.351
In that short 1960 text, Hartman argued

00:11:13.351 --> 00:11:16.497
that French writer, Andre Maurois, quote,

00:11:16.497 --> 00:11:19.667
"Novel show that he was one
of the first to consider art

00:11:19.667 --> 00:11:23.057
"and the heritage of Western
culture as very special weapons

00:11:23.057 --> 00:11:26.210
"in an era of ideological warfare."

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:29.287
Maurois' aim was to, quote,
"reconstruct faith in humanism

00:11:29.287 --> 00:11:31.847
"lost because humanistic
philosophies," quote,

00:11:31.847 --> 00:11:34.869
"failed to produce politically
effective men of action."

00:11:34.869 --> 00:11:38.060
For Hartman, the key
to reconstructing faith

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:41.140
in the political and intellectual
foundations of humanism

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:43.630
is a new kind of quote, "liberal hero,"

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.040
an intellectual or artist
capable of political action

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.520
to preserve man's free access
to his cultural heritage,

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:53.040
but also capable of withdrawal from action

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.523
when this end is achieved.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.700
Hartman's investment in revised humanism

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:01.090
was then displaced to various outlets.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.350
After denial of tenure at Yale in '61,

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:06.830
he assumed professorship
at the University of Iowa,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.340
but when, in 1967,
Hartman returned to Yale

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.880
for a joint appointment in
the English and Comp Lit,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.510
he returned to a school more
ready for confrontations

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.670
with Christian metaphysics.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:18.240
While aiming to seize authority

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.270
from his literary critical forefathers,

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.360
Bloom himself offered
only clues about his aims,

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.547
as in the revised 1970
preface to his 1961 book,

00:12:27.547 --> 00:12:29.630
"The Visionary Company."

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.280
There, Bloom implicitly
connects Jewish traditions

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:33.640
with the radical Miltonic,

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:36.210
romantic Protestant literary
tradition by showing

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.580
the latter's reverence for the
former's Biblical prophecy.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:41.547
Bloom's romantics articulated, quote,

00:12:41.547 --> 00:12:44.800
"A way of seeing and of
living a more human life."

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.127
Romantic poetry quote,
"either liberated man

00:12:47.127 --> 00:12:50.077
"from his fallen condition
or more compellingly,

00:12:50.077 --> 00:12:52.107
"made him see that this condition

00:12:52.107 --> 00:12:54.730
"as an unimaginative fiction."

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:57.860
Hopeful, restorative and
subversive of his Yale

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.440
New Critical fathers
were Bloom's romantics,

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:03.670
a group who followed
in the poetic footsteps

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:04.963
of the Jewish prophets.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:11.050
Retirements or deaths during
the first half of the 1970s

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.970
of Yale English's senior
members encouraged scholars

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.030
to contest literary humanism.

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.870
The challenges that emerged
in Yale English included Jews

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:22.380
who rose to senior positions,
including Hartman and Bloom,

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:23.483
and of junior rank.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:28.770
Leslie Brisman argued in his
first book, published in 1973,

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.527
quote, "that the romantic
fascination with John Milton

00:13:31.527 --> 00:13:34.217
"is a matter of choice,"
rather than as Bloom had it,

00:13:34.217 --> 00:13:36.020
"anxious obligation."

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.430
Howard Felperin extended
deconstructionist theories

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.683
to the study of Shakespeare in 1972.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:44.690
These pioneering Jews'
rewritings dovetailed

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:47.284
with Jews' theoretical
work to revise humanism

00:13:47.284 --> 00:13:50.950
and were part of a larger
post-60s burst of approaches

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:53.750
that subverted the normativity
of knowledge and culture.

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:59.307
Bloom used theory to blaze
a quite heterodox path.

00:13:59.307 --> 00:14:02.860
"An Anxiety of Influence,"
published in 1973,

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.670
Bloom developed an
Nietzschinized Freudian theory,

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.430
using it to show that
a great poet refracted

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.530
of revered precursors verse

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:15.230
TS Elliot argued that
great poets achieved access

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:17.920
to the literary tradition by
honoring their antecedents,

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:20.487
via quote, "a continual self-sacrifice,

00:14:20.487 --> 00:14:23.210
"a continual extinction of personality,"

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.211
but Bloom's strong poets freed
their own artistic voices

00:14:26.211 --> 00:14:30.200
by misreading and concealing
their debts to their ancestors.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.137
Quote, "The history of
fruitful poetic influence

00:14:33.137 --> 00:14:36.267
"is a history of anxiety
and self saving caricature

00:14:36.267 --> 00:14:40.250
"of distortion, a perverse,
willful revisionism."

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:43.160
In Bloom's psychopoetic
universe, literary history

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:45.140
is a sequence of courteous revision was

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.860
but a naive humanist fantasy.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.120
While Bloom's
de-Christianizing contribution

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:52.180
was a personal vendetta of sorts,

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.160
Hartman's assumed a
more community oriented

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:57.063
and explicitly Jewish direction.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.940
After budget crunches of
the early 1970s and amidst

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.300
the destabilizing attacks
on the Christian identity

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.730
that grounded the identity
of older scholars,

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:09.570
Yale's humanists felt
their community in tatters.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.930
A response to this grim
situation had come in the form

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:15.030
of a council in '73,
which Hartman chaired,

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.433
that addressed the
fragmentation of community.

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:21.170
Yet hopes springs eternal in Yale English,

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:24.080
and discerning onlookers
saw a critical group emerge,

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:28.080
with Hartman rising to
senior rank in Yale English.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.210
Bloom's revisionist theories crystallized

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.563
in "Kabbalah and Criticism" in 1975,

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.473
a text from 1975 derived
from his university-wide

00:15:37.473 --> 00:15:39.483
delivered DeVane Lectures at Yale.

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.258
While most Jews spoke
cautiously about their origins

00:15:43.258 --> 00:15:46.500
in the 1960s and '70s,
here was the first critic

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:49.290
in the English tradition, a
half decade before the rise

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:51.300
of the new Christian
right, and on the heels

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:54.552
of the 1973 Arab-Israeli
war, not simply asserting

00:15:54.552 --> 00:15:57.810
his Jewish identity, but speculating

00:15:57.810 --> 00:15:59.550
that a mystical Jewish tradition rested

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:01.590
at the heart of Western culture.

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:03.610
Bloom theorized that the medieval systems

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:07.810
of Kabbalists Moses Cordovero
and Kabbalist Isaac Luria

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:10.410
showed that all great post-Miltonic poems

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.810
were determined by the
anxiety of influence

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.830
and followed patterns
laid down by the rabbis.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:19.580
Yale critic W.K. Wimsatt
famously equated the works;

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.210
interformal harmony with a Christian moral

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:23.770
and aesthetic worth.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.860
Bloom, though, decreed
poems obtained the illusions

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.470
of metaphysical presence, unity, form,

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:31.757
or even meaning from other poems.

00:16:31.757 --> 00:16:34.170
"Student gossip," undergraduates wrote,

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.630
wrote that Bloom's
DeVane series represents

00:16:36.630 --> 00:16:39.443
his attempts to defy
the New Critics at Yale.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:45.156
Yale's 1977 offer of its
presidency to then Dean at Harvard,

00:16:45.156 --> 00:16:49.240
Henry Rosovsky, symbolized
how far the school had come.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:51.790
The mainstreaming of Jews,
like women and other minorities

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.810
into Yale's humanities
departments also involved

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.730
a countervailing force
against discrimination,

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.020
a merit-based academic culture.

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:03.220
To keep pace with peer
institutions, Yale turned to more

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:06.247
quote-unquote, "objective criteria

00:17:06.247 --> 00:17:07.990
"for appointment and rewards,"

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:11.190
to the benefit of excluded
demographic groups.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:13.137
Jews' uses of theory at Yale intersected

00:17:13.137 --> 00:17:15.460
with the diversification of the faculty

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:17.490
and student population.

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:19.510
Around the same time as
the religious right gained

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:22.360
in prominence, and after
the 1970s resurgence

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:25.580
in the academic study of
Judaism, a growing number of Jews

00:17:25.580 --> 00:17:27.610
earned senior faculty rank status,

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:29.330
the humanities departments at Yale became

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:31.200
invested in religious discourse

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:34.000
and radicalized practices of Judaism.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.850
Hartman's interest
intersected with the push

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:38.520
among Yale faculty, begun in 1978,

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.280
to refurbish the school's
Judaic Studies program.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:43.040
The efforts resulted in the establishment

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.540
of an undergraduate major in 1980.

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:48.000
The prominence of Yale's
Department of Religious Studies,

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:51.023
faculty argue, meant that
the university was quote,

00:17:52.447 --> 00:17:54.597
"obliged to set the pace for effective

00:17:54.597 --> 00:17:58.000
"and responsible graduate
study in the neglected area."

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.690
The subsequently founded
Judaic Studies program

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:02.993
was to pursue humanist goals as quote,

00:18:02.993 --> 00:18:05.697
"The Jewish experience," a proposal reads,

00:18:05.697 --> 00:18:08.327
"is coterminous in space
and time with the experience

00:18:08.327 --> 00:18:10.520
"of Western man in general."

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:12.170
Hartman was appointed co-chairman

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:14.620
of Judaic Studies in early 1981.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:16.507
He thought it was a
quote, "variant of opening

00:18:16.507 --> 00:18:18.667
"the literary cannon
to a learning tradition

00:18:18.667 --> 00:18:20.857
"as old as Rome and Athens."

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:23.951
Yale's Judaic Studies
programs was initiated

00:18:23.951 --> 00:18:26.600
when anti-humanist theories were descended

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.170
in the American academy

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:31.160
while Jews used theory
to revised humanist aims.

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.010
Associated with the development drive

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:36.140
for Judaic Studies was the
funding of an archive at Yale

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.743
to house the Holocaust
Survivors' Film Project.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.490
The project would be institutionalized

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:44.605
by way of psychoanalytic
theories, but it emerged in part

00:18:44.605 --> 00:18:47.650
out of work undertaken
during the mid-1970s

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:50.730
by the New Haven Jewish
community, which in 1977,

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.540
completed a Holocaust monument,

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:55.570
America's first on
public land in New Haven.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:57.710
The nationwide Holocaust Memorial culture

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:00.520
constructed through
editorials, newspaper articles

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:03.310
and correspondence in the
immediate postwar period

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:04.330
had thus begun to shift

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:06.970
to a different mode of public remembrance.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:10.520
New Haven's Holocaust Monument
exemplifies this change.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.180
In the 1960s, the American
public's attention

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:15.550
was only infrequently drawn
to the Jewish community

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.240
of Holocaust survivors.

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:22.180
TV miniseries "Holocaust,"
was the first major TV event

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:23.930
though, it upset survivors who believed

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.150
it massified and
under-theorized representations

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:30.580
of the Holocaust risked
establishing a moral paradigm

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:32.863
more exploitative than educational.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:35.940
Such a (indistinct) view
of televised portrayals

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.845
of the Holocaust informed
a 1979 discussion

00:19:38.845 --> 00:19:41.888
on Channel 8 in New Haven of a documentary

00:19:41.888 --> 00:19:44.920
about the New Haven Holocaust Memorial.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:47.690
Local filmmaker, Laurel
Vlock invited Dori Laub,

00:19:47.690 --> 00:19:51.640
a child survivor and New Haven
psychiatrist, to participate.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:54.090
They speculated that
survivors might be prepared

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:55.663
to document the stories.

00:19:56.709 --> 00:19:58.949
Vlock and Laub began filming four stories.

00:19:58.949 --> 00:20:01.670
And then the two realized
the impact the recordings

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:03.630
would have if shared widely.

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:06.500
An early 1980 proposal to garner support

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:09.980
for the newly established
Holocaust Survivals Film Project

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:12.920
also explained the urgency
behind the recording endeavor.

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.792
Quote, "The overriding
consensus of Jews everywhere

00:20:15.792 --> 00:20:18.287
"is that we must never
forget what happened,

00:20:18.287 --> 00:20:19.697
"that our children must be taught again

00:20:19.697 --> 00:20:21.617
"and again, to remember the Holocaust,

00:20:21.617 --> 00:20:24.767
"even though they were not
alive during the dreadful era

00:20:24.767 --> 00:20:27.627
"of Nazi Germany, nevertheless,
not enough is being done

00:20:27.627 --> 00:20:30.127
"to record the living
memory of those years.

00:20:30.127 --> 00:20:32.607
"Every survivor who dies without
leaving a meaningful record

00:20:32.607 --> 00:20:35.017
"of his or her experience
in the Holocaust,

00:20:35.017 --> 00:20:37.607
"a record which can be seen
and heard and studied by Jew

00:20:37.607 --> 00:20:40.147
"and non-Jew alike for all time to come

00:20:40.147 --> 00:20:42.877
"constitutes an
immeasurable historical loss

00:20:42.877 --> 00:20:44.777
"for which we can no longer afford."

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:49.060
Situating survivor
testimony as antithetical

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.550
to common representations of the Holocaust

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:54.150
and in distinction to
anti-humanist discourses

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.280
circulating in humanities departments,

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.960
a public act of remembrance was felt

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:59.593
to be of vital importance.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:03.700
The HSFP's revised humanist
ambitions were stressed

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:08.160
during the official
launching on June 28th, 1979

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:09.900
at a press conference in New Haven

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:11.930
that included New Haven mayor,

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:14.020
local Jewish and Christian clergy,

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:16.520
leaders of the New
Haven Jewish Federation,

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:19.804
a representative of the planned

00:21:19.804 --> 00:21:22.393
United States Holocaust Memorial Museum.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.630
The President's Commission
on the Holocaust

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.350
hailed the project.

00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:29.610
Elie Wiesel, the chair of
the President's Commission,

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:30.780
highlighted the cultural link

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.630
between the Jewish community and humanity.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.087
Quote, "The most vital lesson to be drawn

00:21:35.087 --> 00:21:36.517
"is that Auschwitz was possible

00:21:36.517 --> 00:21:39.237
"because the enemy of the
Jewish people in a mankind,

00:21:39.237 --> 00:21:41.087
"and it is always the same enemy,

00:21:41.087 --> 00:21:43.277
"succeeded in splinting human society,

00:21:43.277 --> 00:21:46.937
"nation against nation,
Christian against Jew,

00:21:46.937 --> 00:21:48.630
"young against old."

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:52.000
The recording of testimonies
became an international effort.

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:54.180
When the HSFP found a home at Yale,

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.680
in part due to Geoffrey Hartman,

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.360
its theoretical framework was fleshed out.

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:01.790
Having evaded the direct
impact of the Holocaust,

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:03.230
Hartman felt a responsibility

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:04.993
to help give voice to survivors.

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:07.730
One of the first four Holocaust survivors

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:10.420
Dori Laub interviewed was
Geoffrey's wife, Renee,

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:13.810
who in 1944, was sent to Bergen-Belsen.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:16.530
Hartman's interest was
surely also stirred by Laub,

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:18.520
whose theoretical manner of approaching

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.290
the video tape project intersected

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:23.740
with Hartman's incorporations
throughout the 1970s

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:25.780
of psychoanalytic theories to develop

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:28.730
a distinct psycholinguistics
that addressed

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:32.080
the link between words and psychic wounds.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.463
Laub theorized that trauma
survivors could mend life

00:22:34.463 --> 00:22:36.823
only when trauma was represented.

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:40.380
In the HSFP, Laub's approach,

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:42.890
a clear theoretical
position that responded

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:45.760
to the visual age that
Hartman had written about,

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.350
required listeners to follow the lead

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:50.083
of self-aware survivors.

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:52.630
Fulfilling the project's goal

00:22:52.630 --> 00:22:54.490
would contribute to scholarship as well.

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:57.743
Quote, "Psychoanalytic
work done in survivors,"

00:22:57.743 --> 00:23:00.507
reviewers of a funding application read,

00:23:00.507 --> 00:23:03.117
"is rather scarce when the
magnitude of the phenomenon

00:23:03.117 --> 00:23:06.177
"is taken into account and
the work done was never part

00:23:06.177 --> 00:23:08.620
"of a large systematic study."

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:10.900
Despite the manifest
historical, pedagogical

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:14.290
and scholarly significance,
the larger academic community

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:16.670
only took notice of the HSFP after members

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:20.500
of the Yale community sanctioned
the undertaking in 1981.

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:23.810
While co-chairman of Yale's
new Judaic Studies department,

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:26.930
Hartman helped inaugurate
Yale's Fortunoff Video Archive

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:30.640
of Holocaust testimonies
on November 15th, 1982,

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.190
with the original tapes
formally deposited at Yale.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:37.887
Peter Novic argued in 2000
that during the 1970s, quote,

00:23:37.887 --> 00:23:40.857
"American Jews' anxiety
about Israel's security

00:23:40.857 --> 00:23:42.727
"and their viewing Israel's situation

00:23:42.727 --> 00:23:44.377
"within a Holocaust framework

00:23:44.377 --> 00:23:46.437
"was the single greatest
catalyst of the centering

00:23:46.437 --> 00:23:49.940
"of the Holocaust in American
Jewish consciousness."

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:53.880
An avenue for the positioning
was Yale's Fortunoff Archive,

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.360
and this archive was itself a
result of grassroots efforts

00:23:57.360 --> 00:24:00.060
in New Haven and Jews'
uses of theory to create

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:02.980
a Jewish humanist moment
at Yale that altered

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:05.030
and created intellectual spaces for Jews

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:06.733
and Jewish history and culture.

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:12.623
President Reagan habitually invoked,

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:17.890
invoked Judeo-Christian values as elements

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:20.620
in the struggle against
communism and/or the glue

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:23.360
that held America together.

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:27.340
For different purposes, editors
of the 1985 inaugural issue

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:29.840
of "Orim," a Jewish journal at Yale,

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:32.670
assessed the Jewish
community's Yale existence.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.154
Quote, "When a great
university," they wrote,

00:24:36.154 --> 00:24:38.907
"becomes an important
center of Jewish learning,

00:24:38.907 --> 00:24:41.975
"the event is in itself a
profound commentary on the nature

00:24:41.975 --> 00:24:44.637
"of the American Jewish experience today."

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:47.930
Great universities were now institutions

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:50.050
where the once virtual
identification of humanism

00:24:50.050 --> 00:24:52.530
with Christian culture had been shattered

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:54.290
with Jewish experiences and religious

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:56.640
and poetic traditions embraced.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.970
Yale president A. Bartlett Giamatti,

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:02.090
former professor of
Renaissance Literature,

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:04.160
threw his support behind the archive,

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:07.380
while Jewish, cultural and
religious life cohered at Yale.

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:10.463
Yale Hillel and Young Israel
House were working together.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.770
Reflecting the new
unity, Orim's commentary

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:16.340
on the link between the contemporary

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:18.900
and earlier American Jewish experience

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.300
also included a section on Midrash,

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:22.980
the classical rabbinic tradition.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.607
Quote, "Our grandfathers and
fathers had their stories

00:25:26.607 --> 00:25:29.377
"of affliction, hope, escape, labor,

00:25:29.377 --> 00:25:32.933
"but above all, of attachment
to Talmud and Midrash."

00:25:32.933 --> 00:25:35.207
"Orim" did not turn to
these quote-unquote,

00:25:35.207 --> 00:25:38.300
"resonating words" simply to
interpret the past, however.

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:41.417
Quote, "Even in the new
tales of visions and triumph

00:25:41.417 --> 00:25:44.377
"in alien worlds, the ancient word threads

00:25:44.377 --> 00:25:47.457
"could still be discerned with
which a God obsessed people

00:25:47.457 --> 00:25:50.847
"had tried unendingly to
lend form and substance

00:25:50.847 --> 00:25:53.940
"to the intuitions of
his collective soul."

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.150
Speculation on how ancient
word threads shaped

00:25:56.150 --> 00:25:58.640
the modern Jewish-American
experience was part

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:02.781
of academics' theorization
going on then about rhetoric,

00:26:02.781 --> 00:26:05.291
that itself, due in part to the popularity

00:26:05.291 --> 00:26:07.353
of what is known as French theory.

00:26:08.600 --> 00:26:10.280
American literary critics sympathized

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:12.810
with the rabbinic theories
of language, because they too

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:16.540
cared for quote, "every element
of scripture as meaningful."

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.840
By the early 1980s, the quote,
"Midrash theory connection"

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:22.740
had become a hot topic
in literary studies.

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.170
Contributing to a project
on literature and Midrash

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:27.200
at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem,

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:30.100
Hartman applied Midrashic
deconstructive theories

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:31.650
to the textual fault lines

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.533
of Jacob's wrestling with the angel.

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:38.280
Scratched underneath the names listed

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.740
on Orim's Yale Faculty Advisory Board

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.520
reveals the scope of Jews
uses of Midrashic theory

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:45.210
to advance their humanism.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.290
The board included poet, John Hollander.

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:51.057
In the '80s, Hollander
theorized that poetry, quote,

00:26:51.057 --> 00:26:53.787
"In the West was like the
rabbinic textual tradition."

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:59.107
In 1988 article, he wrote, quote,

00:26:59.107 --> 00:27:02.567
"All poetry is unofficial Midrash.

00:27:02.567 --> 00:27:05.107
"At the very beginning of the
Western literary tradition

00:27:05.107 --> 00:27:07.787
"were Homer and Torah,
but the great poetry

00:27:07.787 --> 00:27:10.777
"that followed them became
part of the canon as well."

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:16.030
Alan Goodman had in 1972 sought answers

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:19.220
as to why there were not more
major American Jewish poets

00:27:19.220 --> 00:27:21.590
in the English literary tradition.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:23.680
While Hollinger's colleague, Bloom,

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:26.870
believed in the early 1970s
that American Jewish poetry

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:30.090
had yet to truly mature, he, in 1988,

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:34.500
read Hollander's "Spectral
Emanations" collection from 1978

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:37.487
as quote, "Somber American
Jewish mythmaking,

00:27:37.487 --> 00:27:39.760
"founded on the metaphor of menorah."

00:27:39.760 --> 00:27:42.224
For Bloom, this poetry resolved

00:27:42.224 --> 00:27:45.270
the Jewish-American
contradiction by combining

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:48.960
a sacred strain and a late
romantic worldly passion.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.330
And yet, some at Yale English
members still found it curious

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:54.550
that stars in the
department were interested

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:56.900
in Jewish modes of reading.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:01.900
In 1986, a New York Times
writer quoted a lapsed Baptist

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.582
in Yale English who thought it odd that,

00:28:04.582 --> 00:28:07.517
in his department, there were, quote,

00:28:07.517 --> 00:28:10.047
"Jews who have found
medieval Midrash and Kabbalah

00:28:10.047 --> 00:28:13.057
"more or less compelling
modes of interpretation."

00:28:14.093 --> 00:28:17.190
The manner of Jews' contributions

00:28:17.190 --> 00:28:19.250
to the theoretical humanist movement

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:23.300
were nominally different in
the French department at Yale.

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:24.800
There, Shoshana Felman,

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:27.400
also on Orim's Faculty Advisory Board,

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:28.560
explored the connections

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:32.390
between Holocaust survivor
testimonies and French theory.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:33.670
Felman received degrees

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:37.460
at the Hebrew University
of Jerusalem, and in 1970,

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:39.300
earned her PhD in French literature

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.720
at the University of Grenoble.

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:43.610
Felman then joined the French department

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:47.453
and in 1973 became its
first tenured woman and Jew.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.070
In the late 1980s, she became
a member of a circle at Yale

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:55.430
comprised of Hartman, Dori
Laub, Cathy Carruth and others

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:59.310
formed around the concept of testimony.

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:01.180
Culminating the interdisciplinary work

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:04.150
in psychiatric theories that centered

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:06.410
on the Fortunoff Video Archive,

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.920
Felman labored with
Yale to produce in 1992,

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:12.870
a key text in the field of
traumatic mental health,

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:14.813
and a definitive work on testimony.

00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:18.500
In it, Laub wrote about
his work as a psychoanalyst

00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:20.930
with Holocaust survivors while Felman used

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:25.930
autobiographical accounts of
Holocaust and war survivors,

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:29.700
as well as art, literature,
and videos to explore the link

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:32.393
between trauma and pedagogy.

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:36.260
Holocaust survivor testimonies
have become touchstones

00:29:36.260 --> 00:29:39.730
for TV representations of
the Holocaust in the 1980s,

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.320
but in the 1990s,
Felman's and others' work

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:44.690
helped secure the video
archive's reputation

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.910
by offering a theoretical complexity

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:48.500
and Jewish underpinnings that aided

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:50.423
the establishment of trauma studies.

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:55.430
By the mid 1990s, in fact,
Jewish literary critics

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:57.690
and scholars who used
theory to challenge humanism

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:00.070
had reached the pinnacle
of the profession.

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.470
As for this Jewish
movement's local effects,

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:03.900
the chauvinism in Yale English

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.470
was left to historians to explore,

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:09.096
while the de-Christianization
of literary studies there

00:30:09.096 --> 00:30:13.410
interconnected with the
establishment of Jewish spaces.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:17.740
In the 1980s and 1990s,
Yale's Judaic Studies program

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:20.290
exposed future leaders and clergy

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:22.870
to an appreciation of Jewish traditions.

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:26.060
Yale served as a path through
which Jews and Jewish texts

00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:29.230
and culture was shown as
integral to the larger society.

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:31.880
When the third academic generation Jews

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.600
earned professorships and
positions across America,

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:37.660
becoming presidents of the
Modern Language Association,

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:40.797
directors and editors of literary
histories and anthologies,

00:30:40.797 --> 00:30:42.570
Judaic Studies' cultural work--

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:43.618
(static crackling)

00:30:43.618 --> 00:30:46.010
background that it helped construct.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:49.474
One legacy linked to Jews'
deployments of theory at Yale,

00:30:49.474 --> 00:30:52.651
witness and survivor's accounts recorded

00:30:52.651 --> 00:30:56.540
for the Fortunoff Video Archive
for Holocaust testimonies,

00:30:56.540 --> 00:30:59.150
contributed to making the
Holocaust a central reference

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.450
in American culture and
modern Jewish history.

00:31:02.320 --> 00:31:05.477
Consider Lawrence
Langer's book, 1991 book,

00:31:05.477 --> 00:31:09.310
"Holocaust Testimonies,"
which the 1995 issue

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:13.240
of the New York Times
Review of Books listed

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:16.740
as one of the century's
100 most important books.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:18.960
Based on eight years of
studying oral testimonies

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:22.340
held in the Fortunoff Archive,
Langer's study theorized

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:25.507
that created, quote, "An
impromptu self in the camps

00:31:25.507 --> 00:31:27.630
"in order to remain alive."

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:30.510
A 27-minute piece for
string quartet and tape,

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:34.370
Steve Reich's "Different Trains"
from 1995 creates melodies

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:37.730
from looped spoken phrases
from the Fortunoff Archive.

00:31:37.730 --> 00:31:39.340
These phrases were all reminisces

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:41.210
about their radically
different experiences

00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:43.210
during the second World War.

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:45.340
The piece won a 1990 Grammy award

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:47.690
in the classical music category.

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:50.660
In these ways and other,
the Fortunoff Video Archive

00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:53.990
thus played a role in the
Americanization of the Holocaust.

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:55.730
When filmmaker Stephen Spielberg

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:58.520
and the Shoah Foundation prepared
to launch a more extensive

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:02.116
Holocaust testimony in the
mid-1990s, they sought the help

00:32:02.116 --> 00:32:06.370
of Fortunoff Archive staff,
including Hartman, Dana Klein,

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:08.568
and Joanne Wiener Rudolf.

00:32:08.568 --> 00:32:13.300
The video archive informs Spielberg's goal

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:15.600
to offer an education
and provide blueprints

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:18.020
for potential action about the Holocaust

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:19.720
and other genocides.

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:23.660
Providing an education
met a widely felt need

00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:26.750
in multicultural America
for a common moral contract

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:28.823
of a broadly held ethical paradigm.

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:32.310
Historicizing how Jews
employed theory at Yale

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:35.850
to institute a revised
humanist movement and mood

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.190
helps in the understanding
of how the university

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:40.960
became a center of Jewish
and humanist learning.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:43.276
This work can be seen as but
one chapter in the larger

00:32:43.276 --> 00:32:46.063
branch of theory in the American academy,

00:32:47.140 --> 00:32:50.150
a story of how Jews in
concert with feminist studies,

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:52.560
post-colonial studies, ethnic studies,

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:54.630
and other scholars helped decenter

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:56.980
the Christian European perspective.

00:32:56.980 --> 00:32:59.350
Yet what remains certain
is that Yale's Jews,

00:32:59.350 --> 00:33:01.700
who in mid-century felt
excluded from scholarship

00:33:01.700 --> 00:33:03.435
and the profession, partook in an informal

00:33:03.435 --> 00:33:05.900
collective intellectual enterprise

00:33:05.900 --> 00:33:08.577
at once distinctive to the
situation in history of Jews

00:33:08.577 --> 00:33:11.990
and Yale, and in some
ways, illustrative of Jews

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:13.440
and the increasing Jewish presence

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:16.470
in American arts and
humanities departments.

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:18.730
In addition to the de-Christianization
of literary studies

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:22.580
during the '60s and '70s,
Yale's humanist Jews'

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.270
uses of theory helped
create Jewish avenues

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:28.530
in the late '70s and '80s
for the public remembrance

00:33:28.530 --> 00:33:32.310
of the Holocaust, influencing
modern American identity

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.280
and modern Jewish identity.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:36.760
Uncovering a theoretical
path at Yale through which

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:39.550
the Holocaust rose to
prominence in scholarship

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:43.740
and in public awareness of
Jewish history and culture,

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:46.362
this history of Jews,
theory and Yale ultimately

00:33:46.362 --> 00:33:49.350
might help illuminate
the constructive ways

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:52.200
in which it became foregrounded.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:53.890
Thank you all for listening.

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:58.080
And I look forward to
hearing your questions.

00:33:58.080 --> 00:33:58.913
Thank you.

00:33:58.913 --> 00:33:59.746
- Okay.

00:33:59.746 --> 00:34:00.980
Thank you, Greg.

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:02.710
I think we have a lot to talk about.

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:05.310
That was a really interesting talk,

00:34:05.310 --> 00:34:09.880
and there are a lot,
already a lot of questions

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:14.720
and just looking at the list
of people who are attending,

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:19.460
I think there are people who
were part of that history here.

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:21.083
So one thing just off,

00:34:22.084 --> 00:34:27.030
to start, I was interested
and happy to hear you say

00:34:27.030 --> 00:34:29.440
that the French department was,

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:33.340
I'm a professor of French,
and was a little bit different

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:36.180
from the English department.

00:34:36.180 --> 00:34:41.180
I would say, that Felman was
not the first tenured Jew

00:34:41.970 --> 00:34:44.670
in the French department.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:46.990
Before her, there was Georges May,

00:34:46.990 --> 00:34:49.800
who was a very important
figure on the Yale campus,

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:53.440
and who was Dean in 1968,
Dean of the college,

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:58.440
which was a very, and
Victor Brombert also,

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:00.190
I think, was before.

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:01.987
- I just wanted to-
- Yeah.

00:35:01.987 --> 00:35:05.290
- So I meant woman.
- Oh, the first woman tenured.

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:07.027
Right, okay, the first, got it.

00:35:07.027 --> 00:35:11.324
But it is interesting
that the French department

00:35:11.324 --> 00:35:14.160
would've been more, open,

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.470
and I'm wondering like, I mean,

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:19.870
this is my own kind of interest here, but,

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:22.440
do you think that that's because

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:27.459
of a certain kind of
intellectual tradition in France

00:35:27.459 --> 00:35:30.749
versus the United States,

00:35:30.749 --> 00:35:33.043
is that a legacy of French universalism

00:35:33.043 --> 00:35:37.740
being sort of theoretically
more open to Jews?

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:40.130
Or is it a sociological thing,

00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:44.940
if there were just a lot
of Jews who studied French,

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:48.256
like George May and Brombert,
who were European refugees,

00:35:48.256 --> 00:35:52.081
or maybe they weren't
even perceived as Jews,

00:35:52.081 --> 00:35:55.200
as much as people in the English
department would've been,

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:56.850
they were just foreign, you know?

00:35:58.129 --> 00:36:03.129
- So, well, I think that
it's the legacy, okay,

00:36:03.920 --> 00:36:05.750
obviously there are many
factors that go into this,

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:06.990
but if I have to give an answer,

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:11.670
I would say that the legacy
in this case would be,

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:16.670
I imagine just that the
legacy or the culture

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:21.740
in Yale English developed,

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:26.699
has deep traditions in American culture.

00:36:26.699 --> 00:36:28.990
And I think that you're right to emphasize

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:30.670
the French emphasis on universalism

00:36:30.670 --> 00:36:32.640
and the emigre scholars as well.

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:34.501
I think were, perhaps, I mean,

00:36:34.501 --> 00:36:36.430
you would know more about this,

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:39.670
perhaps more prominent in
French than in the cases

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:42.270
of Yale English, where those
scholars were, perhaps,

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:45.733
coming from Anglo-American,

00:36:47.237 --> 00:36:50.445
like let's say an
Anglo-American pool, let's say.

00:36:50.445 --> 00:36:55.445
And so, I think that
that lent and inculcated

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:59.280
a certain culture in the department.

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:04.280
At the very least, it created
a intellectual culture

00:37:04.906 --> 00:37:09.906
that scholars such as Hartman
and Bloom were very aware of

00:37:09.930 --> 00:37:14.520
and sensitive to, and
so, perhaps the cultures

00:37:14.520 --> 00:37:17.080
were not as stark.

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:20.155
I mean, I would need to
do more work, however,

00:37:20.155 --> 00:37:24.410
from the perspective of, let's say,

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:26.980
American Jews studying,
especially in this case,

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:29.670
the romanticism, right, in Yale English,

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:31.440
there's all this, it's all loaded

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:36.440
with these meanings,
implicit and explicit.

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:39.480
- Yeah, it makes me think
of a book that I really like

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:43.850
a lot, Jonathan Friedman's
book, "The Temple of Culture,"

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:47.950
where he talks about how,
the very idea of culture

00:37:47.950 --> 00:37:51.000
in the Anglo-American
context was, we're thinking

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:53.560
about Matthew Arnold and
people like that is theorized,

00:37:53.560 --> 00:37:56.561
as a kind of opposition to a Hebraicism,

00:37:56.561 --> 00:38:00.720
and that is, there is
that current, I think,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.480
within French culture too,

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:05.270
but it's maybe not the dominant one.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:08.010
So okay, so one thing that I,
a question that immediately

00:38:08.010 --> 00:38:12.120
leapt out at me, and I
see some of the people

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.120
in the audience, too,
in one of your pictures,

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:15.656
there was, of the French,

00:38:15.656 --> 00:38:18.440
I think it was Lacan's lecture,

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:22.130
there's Paul De Man is in the picture,

00:38:22.130 --> 00:38:26.050
and it seems like he's a kind
of key part of this story,

00:38:26.050 --> 00:38:28.580
and wondering if you can
say a little bit more

00:38:28.580 --> 00:38:30.610
about his role in this.

00:38:30.610 --> 00:38:33.730
And how does that fit into your argument

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:38.730
about the kind of humanist,
contesting humanism,

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:41.780
where does his brand of deconstruction,

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:43.820
maybe just maybe tell us a little bit

00:38:43.820 --> 00:38:46.360
for those who might not
know, who was De Man

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.273
and what was the scandal?

00:38:49.248 --> 00:38:54.248
- So Paul De Man, a leading
figure in what is known

00:38:55.578 --> 00:39:00.475
as literary deconstruction
or deconstruction in Comp Lit

00:39:00.475 --> 00:39:03.270
and French at Yale, and
beginning in, I believe,

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:06.740
he was hired in 1970, but
he began teaching in '71

00:39:08.320 --> 00:39:11.933
and until his death in '83, and then,

00:39:14.535 --> 00:39:17.184
so his reputation of his
theory was essentially

00:39:17.184 --> 00:39:20.593
the reputation, which in my opinion is,

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:24.430
well, the reputation is
that his literary theory

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:28.750
was explicitly anti-human
and nihilistic, almost,

00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:32.733
very destructive of let's
say the core humanist values

00:39:32.733 --> 00:39:35.620
that let's say college
is supposed to teach.

00:39:35.620 --> 00:39:39.690
And in 1987, 4 years after his death,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:44.690
it was discovered that he wrote
approximately 200 articles

00:39:44.854 --> 00:39:48.990
when he was 20 and 21 years old in Europe.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:52.280
He was a Belgian, so he
is a Belgian-American,

00:39:52.280 --> 00:39:54.872
and two of them were, I would,

00:39:54.872 --> 00:39:58.820
explicitly antisemitic, whereas
the vast majority of them

00:39:58.820 --> 00:40:01.550
were culturally, at the
very least sympathetic

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:06.550
to the German dominant, the
Nazi dominance of Europe.

00:40:07.130 --> 00:40:08.784
And this was very controversial.

00:40:08.784 --> 00:40:12.233
He was very close friends
with both Harold Bloom

00:40:12.233 --> 00:40:17.233
and Geoffrey Hartman, who had
their own different responses

00:40:18.016 --> 00:40:21.430
to the controversy, but they were a part

00:40:21.430 --> 00:40:24.800
of an intellectual cohort
at Yale that were pursuing

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:26.990
deconstructive theories, essentially.

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:27.823
Yeah.

00:40:29.230 --> 00:40:31.830
- Okay, there are a few
questions I'm seeing

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:36.830
from the audience about
the concept of humanism

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.810
and anti-humanism.

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:42.897
Someone writes, "I'm
uncomfortable with the language

00:40:42.897 --> 00:40:43.997
"of anti-humanism.

00:40:43.997 --> 00:40:45.997
